  "recovery_latency_s": 174.67400217056274,
  "client_view_text": " Recovery\nA write-ahead log, also known as a redo log or write log, is a mechanism used by databases to ensure data consistency and recoverability in the event of a crash or system failure. When a database transaction is committed, the database first writes the transaction's changes to the write-ahead log before updating the main database files. This ensures that the transaction's effects are permanently recorded, even if the database crashes before the changes are written to the main files.\nIf the database crashes before all its changes are written to the main files, the write-ahead log serves as a record of all the transactions that were in progress when the crash occurred. When the database restarts, it can use the write-ahead log to redo the transactions that were not completed, bringing the database back to a consistent state before the crash. This process is called recovery.\nThe write-ahead log is essentially a journal of all the changes made to the database, which allows the database to recover its state by replaying the transactions recorded in the log. This ensures that the database can recover to a consistent and up-to-date state, even if the system crashed or was shut down abruptly. The write-ahead log also helps to prevent data loss and ensures data consistency by ensuring that all transactions are properly committed or rolled back"
}